    "surface": "newheap-db direct schema and JSON query contracts plus consumer-owned governed schema, indexes and query MCP tools",
    "outcome": "Developers and agents take the repository-declared database fast path without inspecting application code, settings, secrets or package internals to rediscover routing or JSON fields: one bounded tracked-file search finds a nested catalog, its directory becomes the diagnostic working directory, and its only profile automatically selects the provider, configuration path, connection-string name, ceilings and default environment. Local `validate` opens no connection and advertises the `outbound-network` capability required by `schema` and `query`; Codex starts those execution commands with a narrow reusable `newheap-db` network permission instead of first probing its restricted sandbox. When the user explicitly requests another runtime environment, `--environment` applies it consistently to schema and query without requiring a duplicate profile solely for Production or Staging; the governed connection-string name and read-only-principal verification remain unchanged. `newheap-db` resolves that name through the selected environment's normal configuration and secrets flow without exposing the connection string. A direct `schema --search ... --describe-if-single` invocation needs no JSON schema request and returns a unique match plus its exact columns, indexes and relationships through one connection and read-only transaction; zero or multiple matches remain bounded summaries. `schema` and `query` validate internally, so interactive diagnostics do not spend a separate call on `validate`. The executable sample prompt uses Production from a Development-default profile, counts projects and returns the latest by `CreationDateTime` with exactly one schema invocation and one query invocation. Checked-in schema and query requests remain repeatable CI and dry-run contracts. Permission-filtered exact descriptions expose named outgoing and incoming relationships with ordered column pairs and validation status, while at most one focused index lookup may inform an immediately useful predicate or ordering. Structured MCP input and the direct CLI `--request-file` option keep serialized JSON out of Windows process arguments. The executable sample MCP fixes profile, capability, a sixteen-call budget and ceilings server-side, requires an explicit requested row count, rejects excessive requests before execution, and fails without partial data when actual results exceed the accepted limit, while SQL Server and PostgreSQL enforce dedicated read-only credentials and return only safe provider classifications, codes, stages and retry hints. SQL Server principal rejection also returns an optional first rejected verificationCheck such as function-select; the MCP preserves allowlisted checks without exposing principal or object names, tolerates older responses without the field, and leaves the permission policy unchanged. Unknown connection failures become transient `connection-failed` outcomes without weakening `authentication-failed` or `database-not-found`, and no server name, credential or provider exception text reaches the response. An unresolved NewHeap secret returns `connection-string-unresolved` with the process-local secrets-path remediation, while malformed resolved provider configuration returns `connection-configuration-invalid`; neither exposes its value or produces schema or query evidence. A checked-in exact local path may be applied to the `newheap-db` child process for one schema retry without permanently changing Production settings. Every other non-success governed schema response ends the diagnostic attempt, so agents do not infer deployed identifiers, relationships, enum values or business semantics from source code or a repository index and do not issue a data query.",
